Answer":"E: 1/4","reason":"Let q_n be the probability that all three bin parities agree after n balls. Then q_0=1 and q_(n+1)=(1-q_n)/3: a mixed-parity state has exactly one bin whose toggle makes all parities agree. Hence q_n=1/4+(3/4)(-1/3)^n. For n=2023, agreement means all odd, and the probability is (1-3^(-2022))/4, closest to 1/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["Uses the usual interpretation that placements are independent and each bin is equally likely."],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"binomial-incidence-count","label":"Count assignments and odd-bin incidences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"binomial-incidence-count":["cnm_h240","math:double-counting"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h240","math:double-counting"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-odd-bin-incidences","label":"Count odd bins across all assignments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-odd-bin-incidences":["cnm_h137","cnm_h240","math:double-counting","cnm_p156"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h137","cnm_h240","math:double-counting","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 1/4, under independent uniform placement","reason":"Let p_n be the probability that all three occupancies have the same parity. Then p_0=1 and p_(n+1)=(1−p_n)/3, since from a mixed parity state only choosing the exceptional bin makes all parities agree. Thus p_n=1/4+(3/4)(−1/3)^n. For odd n=2023 this is the all-odd event, with probability (1−3^(−2022))/4, closest to 1/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["The placement law is not explicit. The answer assumes each ball independently chooses each bin with probability 1/3; without a placement law, the literal probability is not determined."],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"polynomial-parity-filter","label":"Extract odd occupancies from a generating polynomial","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polynomial-parity-filter":["math:generating-functions","cnm_h240"]},"nodeIds":["math:generating-functions","cnm_h240"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-odd-bin-incidences","label":"Count assignments and odd bins","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-odd-bin-incidences":["cnm_h137","cnm_h240","math:double-counting","cnm_p156"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h137","cnm_h240","math:double-counting","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2","reason":"If 5 divides a, then a^100 is divisible by 125 and has remainder 0. Otherwise gcd(a,125) = 1, and Euler's theorem gives a^100 ≡ 1 modulo 125 because φ(125) = 125-25 = 100. Both remainders occur, for example at a = 5 and a = 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-and-expand","label":"Square first, then expand","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-expand":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h240","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h240","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, -29","reason":"After m transitions the row sum is 3·2^m. Since 12288=3·2^12, m=12. The second entry after j transitions is 3-j, so the third entry is 1+∑_{j=0}^{11}(3-j)=1+36-66=-29.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"binomial-coefficients","label":"Expand the row polynomial","methodDirectNodeIds":{"binomial-coefficients":["cnm_h240","cnm_h241"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h240","cnm_h241"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"3","reason":"The x^2 term is C(3,2)x^2 = 3x^2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h240_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h240","cnm_h237"],"id":"identify-binomial-term","label":"Identify the binomial coefficient","methodDirectNodeIds":{"identify-binomial-term":["cnm_h241"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h241","cnm_h240","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"16","reason":"The coefficient sum is (1+1)^4 = 2^4 = 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h242_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h240","cnm_029"],"id":"m1","label":"Set both variables to one","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h242"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h242","cnm_h240","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"16","reason":"C(4,0)+C(4,1)+C(4,2)+C(4,3)+C(4,4) = 1+4+6+4+1 = 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h242_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h240","cnm_020"],"id":"cnm_h242_q4_m1","label":"Sum of binomial coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_h242_q4_m1":["cnm_h242"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h242","cnm_h240","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, -21","reason":"The term indexed by k is (-1)^k C(7,k)a^(7-3k/2). Exponent -1/2 requires k = 5, giving coefficient -C(7,5) = -21.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h240","cnm_h039","cnm_h237"],"id":"match-binomial-term-exponent","label":"Find the required binomial term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-binomial-term-exponent":["cnm_h241"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h241","cnm_h240","cnm_h039","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 0","reason":"Evaluating the polynomial at x=y=1 sums all numerical coefficients, giving (1-2+1)^7=0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"rewrite-binomial","label":"Rewrite the perfect square then use its binomial expansion","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rewrite-binomial":["cnm_148","cnm_h240","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_h240","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, [81,242]; more specifically, 81≤m≤160","reason":"Let e_n=x_n-4, so e_0=1 and e_(n+1)=e_n*(e_n+9)/(e_n+10). Inductively 0<e_(n+1)<e_n<=1, whence 9/10<e_(n+1)/e_n<=10/11. Induction gives (9/10)^n<=e_n<=(10/11)^n. Since (9/10)^4>1/2, e_80>2^(-20); since (10/11)^8<1/2, e_160<2^(-20). The first crossing therefore satisfies 81<=m<=160, inside C. Reuse the complete rational simplification, inequality, recurrence, geometric term and induction route. This explicitly derives the comparison rather than broadening simple recurrence generation to a nonlinear comparison theorem or creating another synthesis node.","recordedAnswer":"C","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-error-bounds","label":"Bound the excess by geometric sequences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-error-bounds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","cnm_h213","cnm_h240"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","cnm_h213","cnm_h240"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, [81,242]","reason":"Set y_n=x_n−4. Then y_0=1 and y_{n+1}=y_n(y_n+9)/(y_n+10), so y_n decreases and (9/10)^n<y_n≤(10/11)^n for n>0. Since (9/10)^4>1/2 and (10/11)^8<1/2, y_80>2^(−20) and y_160<2^(−20). Thus 81≤m≤160, within choice C.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-bounds","label":"Bound the excess above four","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-bounds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","cnm_096","cnm_h240"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","cnm_096","cnm_h240"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a5169760c44ddad1c2987dd0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 68","reason":"Put u=sin²(π/8) and v=cos²(π/8), so u+v=1 and uv=1/8. Grouping the complementary tangent pairs makes the expression (4/u-2)(4/v-2)=8/(uv)+4=68.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h240"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"complex-powers-and-coefficients","label":"Use a polynomial for squared tangents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complex-powers-and-coefficients":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h240","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h064"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","